It's natural to be concerned about the respondent's well-being, given their seemingly distressing response.

While the consent form did state that the survey was anonymous, there's a grey area when respondents provide identifiable details in their free responses.&#x20;

In this situation, it's not necessary to need to follow up with the student. Prioritize the respondent's autonomy and consent.

If you're concerned about the student's well-being, you could consider reaching out to your university's counseling services or health services to report the situation anonymously and seek guidance on how to handle it.



The FERPA guidelines can be referenced to see how they may or may not apply in this situation.&#x20;
